  {
   "id": "wtLkpNy6CwAJ",
   "from": "Vincent van Hees",
   "author": "017566be30afa7f3",
   "date": "2024-11-26T15:08:52Z",
   "subject": "Re: [GGIR] Re: Code for non-wear detection",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\">Hi Khadija,</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\"><br></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\">I see I never replied to your message...</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\"><br></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\">The narrative documentation on nonwear can be found here:</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\"><span><a rel=\"noreferrer nofollow noopener\" href=\"https://wadpac.github.io/GGIR/articles/chapter3_QualityAssessment.html#non-wear-detection\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://wadpac.github.io/GGIR/articles/chapter3_QualityAssessment.html%23non-wear-detection&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792488853456000&amp;usg=AOvVaw22MHz4UrlUvVXSU9jiZr6Y\">https://wadpac.github.io/GGIR/articles/chapter3_QualityAssessment.html#non-wear-detection</a></span><br></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\"><br></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\">The first stage of nonwear detection happens in GGIR part 1, which essentially entails labelling each 60 minute window with a nonwear score to reflect the number of axes that meet the nonwear criteria. The essential code can be found here: <a rel=\"noreferrer nofollow noopener\" href=\"https://github.com/wadpac/GGIR/blob/master/R/detect_nonwear_clipping.R\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/wadpac/GGIR/blob/master/R/detect_nonwear_clipping.R&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792488853456000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3G8_ub8uVEdrQJpHtfB-ae\">https://github.com/wadpac/GGIR/blob/master/R/detect_nonwear_clipping.R</a></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\"><br></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\">The second stage of the nonwear detection is to filter short lasting wear surrounded by a lot of wear and to collapse the nonwearscore into a simple binary classification, which happens in GGIR part 2 and more specifically in </div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\"><span><a rel=\"noreferrer nofollow noopener\" href=\"https://github.com/wadpac/GGIR/blob/master/R/g.weardec.R\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/wadpac/GGIR/blob/master/R/g.weardec.R&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792488853456000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0VZkvDevXJHJb3zBZQ7sfl\">https://github.com/wadpac/GGIR/blob/master/R/g.weardec.R</a></span></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\">which is called from <span><a rel=\"noreferrer nofollow noopener\" href=\"https://github.com/wadpac/GGIR/blob/master/R/g.impute.R\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/wadpac/GGIR/blob/master/R/g.impute.R&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792488853456000&amp;usg=AOvVaw06M_gVAfno19-Le4WVYAQ2\">https://github.com/wadpac/GGIR/blob/master/R/g.impute.R</a>.</span></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\"><br></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\">... I know function names and structure could be improved but due to lack of resources priority has been elsewhere.</div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\"><br></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\">Best wishes,</div>\r\n<div style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\">\r\n    <div>\r\n        <div><div><br></div><div>Vincent</div></div></div></div><div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div>\r\n        On Tuesday, 14 May 2024 at 4:23 PM, Michael Rueschman &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">michael....@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br>\r\n        </div>"
